WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.280
DAVID TU: Hey, this is David Tu.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.830
And in this video, I'll be
walking through new launches

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.810
for monitoring and telemetry.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.487
Stick around to learn about
Service Directory and Network

00:00:09.487 --> 00:00:10.320
Intelligence Center.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.694
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.900
First off is something I'm
really excited to share.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.650
Service Directory provides a
managed runtime service catalog

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.020
that allows all of a
customer's services

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.540
to be accessed from a
single pane of glass

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.830
across Google Cloud, on-prem,
and hybrid environments.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.080
With Service Directory,
you have the ability

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.820
to look up services.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.670
Services can use Service
Directory to look each other up

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.800
by name, instead
of needing to know

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.920
infrastructure-level
details, like IP addresses.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.390
Service Directory can be
queried via HTTP, GRPC, and DNS.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.520
Service Directory also
gives you the ability

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.260
to catalog services.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.020
Developers can annotate and
search for custom properties

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.170
on their services.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.790
For example, if I'm trying to
make a service GDPR-compliant,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.520
I might also want to use
this information dynamically

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.890
to have the service
only be allowed

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.340
to communicate with other
GDPR-compliant services.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.340
Now instead of having multiple
Google Cloud APIs determining

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.250
which service is
GDPR-compliant, an admin

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.260
can easily identify
all the services

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.980
that are GDPR-compliant with
the Service Directory API.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.580
To recap, Service Directory
provides a single pane of glass

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.240
that discovers, registers,
and catalogs your services

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.740
for easy management.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.990
The next topic for
monitoring and telemetry

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.240
is the Network
Intelligence Center.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.610
Network Intelligence Center
provides a single console

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.510
for managing Google Cloud
network visibility, monitoring,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.480
and troubleshooting.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.980
There are currently four modules
to the Network Intelligence

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.030
Center--

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.560
Network Topology, Connectivity
Test, Performance Dashboard,

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.500
and Firewall Insights.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.560
Today, we'll cover Network
Topology and Connectivity Test.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.740
For starters, Network Topology
is a visualization tool

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.710
for viewing the topology
of your VPC networks

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.990
and the metrics that are
associated with your Google

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.790
Cloud resources.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.860
Network Topology shows
actual traffic flows

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.780
between various
networking nodes,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.530
including Google Cloud
workloads and network services,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.380
like load balancers
and external clients,

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.320
and associated
performance metrics.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.390
One thing I'm
particularly excited about

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.810
is the ability to view your
infrastructure changes.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.850
You can look at
topology evolution

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.350
and its associated metrics
for six weeks of history.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.990
This interactive
visualization of the topology

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:38.380
helps reduce the time spent
to monitor and troubleshoot

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.060
workflows.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.460
It also helps you optimize
your architecture for better

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.310
performance and/or cost.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.740
Continuing on with the
Network Intelligence Center,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.310
we'll now cover an addition
to the Connectivity Test.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.230
As a refresher,
Connectivity Test

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.690
is a configuration
and route analyzer

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.660
that lets you diagnose
connectivity issues in your VPC

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.130
network due to misconfiguration
or due to missing routes.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.230
When analyzing network
configurations,

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.080
Connectivity Test uses
an abstract state machine

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.160
to model how a VPC network
should process packets.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.870
Google Cloud
processes the packet

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.820
in several logical steps.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.770
With this launch,
Connectivity Test

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.860
can take into account the newly
launched hierarchical firewall

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.990
policy rules, in addition to
the previous VPC firewall rules.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.300
For context, hierarchical
firewall policies

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.550
provide you the ability
to create firewall rules,

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.153
not only at the VPC, but at the
folder and organization level.

00:03:35.153 --> 00:03:36.820
The Connectivity Test
starts by checking

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.160
the hierarchical
firewall policy.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.460
If connectivity is blocked by
a hierarchical firewall policy

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.160
rule, the trace includes
the name of the policy.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.690
If the connectivity is blocked
by a VPC firewall rule,

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.600
the trace lists the name of
the relevant ingress or egress

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.820
firewall rule.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.850
So this concludes our updates
on monitoring and telemetry.

